{"reference":"Hosea 7","book":"Hosea","chapter":7,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Political Crisis and Misplaced Security","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter’s references to kings, courtly intrigue, Egypt, and Assyria can be read against the northern kingdom’s later eighth-century imperial crisis. Its accusations bring together corrupt leadership, public participation in wrongdoing, and unstable foreign dependence: rulers and princes are implicated in lies and violence, while Ephraim turns toward Egypt and Assyria rather than returning to God. This critique concerns toxic power, elite extraction, war, and misplaced security; it should not be turned into a prescription for a particular modern party or state policy.","verse_refs":["Hosea 7:1-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hosea_007_1723ee37b1ea229acf727115"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}